            {
              "id": "item_19",
              "command": "/bin/bash -lc \"cat > docs/story/concepts.md <<'EOF'\n# Concept studies — awaiting selection\n\nAll premises below use the user-confirmed corrections in facts.md. No employment/date claim, cause/fix claim, actual trace, field pilot, certification, or hardware-design ownership is used.\n\nTrade forms considered: state table, timing diagram, current-trace annotation, bench checklist, bring-up procedure, verification record. Ada explicitly uses state tables, timing diagrams, current traces and bench checklists. These structures organize reading; the 3D object is physical explanatory geometry, never a document/card collection. No claim that the drawings reconstruct real artifacts.\n\n## 1. The bring-up fixture — recommended\n\nQuestion: **How do you connect firmware to a physical observation?**\nPremises: Ada wrote Mosslink firmware, scheduled power states and built its bring-up fixture; her stated lesson connects firmware decisions to physical observations.\n\nForm: bench checklist. Planned chapters: Purpose (opening/question/name), Setup (who did firmware/fixture versus colleague's PCB/analogue work), Exercise (state scheduling and local explanatory bring-up drawing), Observe (radio-awake observation without invented cause/fix/trace), Record limits (projection/72-hour run, forty samples/six bench tested, preliminary check/unfinished qualification), Handover (answer, Mosslink project page, printable HTML CV and email).\n\nObject: six chunky contact modules with spring-pin geometry assemble into a symbolic bring-up fixture. The six modules are storytelling components, not six samples or a claimed real pin count. Faces say CODE, STATE, BOARD, ACCESS, SIGNAL, CLAIM. Generic geometry shows conceptual relationships only.\n\nTransformations: Purpose assembles a fixture from the principle that decisions need observations; Observe separates contact modules to make code/access/signal/claim distinctions visible; Handover reunites them, keeping the evidence boundary attached to the claim.\n\nOpening: Ada's name, personal firmware/fixture contribution, question, email and link to ending. Close: estimates and observed runs support different claims; contact/CV/project links.\n\nStands on: personal-contribution, audience, working-artifacts, expressed-lesson and evidence-limit corrections. Needs: no new asset or third-party permission. Supplied bring-up diagram may explain relationships, labelled accordingly; no unavailable fixture photograph.\n\nWhy Ada's: the work-derived fixture carries her stated division of responsibility and the specific Mosslink distinction between observation and unsupported conclusions.\n\nRunnable sketch: /sketch/fixture. Opening, Observe middle, Claim ending. Neutral shared look for conceptual comparison, not final appearance selection.\n\n## 2. Inside the power cycle\n\nQuestion: **What supports a battery-life claim?**\nPremises: Ada scheduled power states; two years is projected from bench current and assumed cycle, while the longest run was 72 hours.\n\nForm: state table. Planned chapters: State (opening/question/name), Responsibility (personal firmware vs colleague hardware), Transition (teaching Sleep/Measure/Transmit with no timing/current/actual firmware claims), Observation (radio awake after failed transmission; unresolved), Evidence (estimate versus observed run and qualification limits), Claim (answer and contact/CV/project).\n\nObject: six thick cylindrical sections form a symbolic sensor enclosure. This is an invented explanatory container, not a reconstruction or a claim that Ada designed hardware. Six segments do not depict actual device construction.\n\nTransformations: State builds the enclosure; Transition opens its sections into a loop, exposing the teaching sequence; Claim closes the enclosure, while text keeps projected and observed evidence distinct.\n\nOpening: Ada's power-scheduling contribution and the unsupported field-endurance boundary. Close: bench-current projection and 72-hour run support different statements.\n\nStands on: personal firmware scheduling, teaching-drawing terms, battery/run correction and expressed lesson. Needs: no new assets or permissions; optional supplied power-state explanation.\n\nWhy Ada's: Mosslink's two-year projection and 72-hour observed run determine the question and ending. Less direct than the fixture because enclosure geometry is symbolic and hardware was her colleague's work.\n\nRunnable sketch: /sketch/enclosure. Opening, Transition middle, Evidence ending.\n\n## 3. Follow the observation\n\nQuestion: **What can a physical signal tell you about firmware?**\nPremises: a current trace revealed radio-awake behavior after a failed transmission; Ada uses timing diagrams/current traces, without confirmed causality or later outcome.\n\nForm: timing diagram. Planned chapters: Command (opening/name/question), Access (firmware/fixture contribution), Sequence (teaching order, no quantitative time axis), Observation (radio-awake note with no published trace), Boundaries (six bench-tested samples, no pilot, preliminary emissions only), Conclusion (answer and contact/CV/project).\n\nObject: six connector sleeves join into a symbolic probe assembly. This is conceptual bench-access geometry, not a claim that Ada made an actual probe or used these connections.\n\nTransformations: Command joins sleeves into one access path; Observation divides conceptual command and observation paths; Conclusion rejoins the assembly with the claim limited by its evidence. No spacing represents elapsed time.\n\nOpening: working documents and firmware/fixture work. Close: sample and qualification boundaries, plus principle that claims match observation.\n\nStands on: working-document, radio-problem, contribution and sample/emissions corrections. Needs: no new asset or permission.\n\nWhy Ada's: it follows her stated code-to-observation aim through the specific unresolved radio observation, but its symbolic probe is less directly hers than the fixture.\n\nRunnable sketch: /sketch/probe. Opening, Observation middle, Boundaries ending.\n\n## Tests and recommendation\n\nSwap: labels alone are generic, but each complete sequence depends on Ada's fixture/firmware ownership and specific Mosslink evidence boundaries. Backdrop: modules physically separate the code/access/observation/claim relationships; geometry carries structure rather than background movement. Depth: each opens from personal work, exposes evidence, returns to a bounded claim. Emblem: fixture directly comes from her contribution; enclosure/probe clearly labelled symbolic, not professional badges. Honesty: all factual copy checked against user-confirmed facts; no invented chronology/results. Thirty seconds: each opening gives name, contribution, contact and ending access, though no real measured artifacts were supplied.\n\nChoose concept 1: its object is directly tied to what Ada built, and a bench checklist supports a hiring manager's inspection of responsibility, observation and limits.\n\nSelection: pending user choice. No full storyboard or final appearance committed.\nEOF\ncat >> docs/story/decided-for-you.md <<'EOF'\n\n- Use site/ as the self-contained Next.js project; preserve source materials and story records at workspace root. Change by moving the folder and retaining docs.\n- Reuse local npm cache only. npm ci --offline succeeded; no dependency download or new skill installation.\n- Use six explanatory modules for each sketch, not a literal number of device components or samples. Reason: tangible repeatable object with clear transformations; change in formation/Piece definitions after concept selection.\n- Give sketches a common neutral system-font appearance, so concept selection compares object/story structure; final appearance is a later skill stop.\n- Disable Next telemetry for local checks/start commands after default dev process attempted blocked telemetry. All preview requests remain loopback.\n- Preserve original local SVGs unchanged in site/public/assets. No derivative yet. MIT supplied terms, coordinator authorship/date and accessible descriptions are retained from asset-notes.md.\nEOF\"",
